In an A.P. we move from one term to the next by adding the same number \(d\). In a G.P. we move by multiplying by the same number \(r\). Three examples:
\(2, 6, 18, 54, \ldots\) (multiply by \(r = 3\)): the terms grow fast;
\(3, -6, 12, -24, \ldots\) (multiply by \(r = -2\)): the signs alternate;
\(16, 8, 4, 2, \ldots\) (multiply by \(r = \frac{1}{2}\)): the terms shrink.
Repeated doubling, compound growth and halving (like the remaining amount of a radioactive substance) all follow this pattern.
